    description: "FOUND 2026-08-29 as collateral while running the io_boundary/taint/catalogue test\nsurface for INV-kanuk. NOT caused by that change: reproduced on a CLEAN tree\n(git stash, run, unstash) at 4c82fe0131.\n\nTHE FAILURE\n    packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/tests/test_js_ts.py::\n      TestNamespaceImports::test_namespace_import_disambiguates_same_name_functions\n\n    Expected call to resolve to a_early/utils.process, but got\n    javascript:.../z_late/utils.js:2-4:process:function\n\nDeterministic on this machine, in isolation and in a wide run alike.\n\nWHY IT IS AN ENVIRONMENT DEPENDENCY AND NOT A RED dev\n  * test_js_ts.py IS in the committed manifest (.ci/affected-tests.txt line\n    303), so CI selects it.\n  * PR #639's CI passed at this same commit, minutes before this failure was\n    observed locally.\n  * Both cannot be true of a deterministic, environment-independent test.\n\nThe fixture names are the tell: `a_early` and `z_late` are chosen so that a\ncorrect resolver picks the first by IMPORT PATH while a resolver that merely\ntakes whatever it enumerated first would pick by NAME ORDER. A test whose\nverdict can flip with directory iteration order is measuring the filesystem as\nmuch as the resolver.\n\nWHAT TO DO (not done here -- filed, not chased)\n  1. Confirm the mechanism: does the assertion track os.listdir / glob order?\n     If so the test needs a deterministic ordering, not a rename.\n  2. Decide which behaviour is actually specified. WI-vurop (done) is the\n     namespace-import work this test guards; if path_hint disambiguation is the\n     contract, the resolver -- not the test -- is what is order-sensitive, and\n     then this is a PRODUCT bug with a test that only sometimes catches it.\n  3. That second reading is the expensive one and is why this is filed rather\n     than silently re-run until green: a test that passes in CI and fails\n     locally is normally blamed on the environment, and here that may be\n     exactly backwards."  # cc65
    fields: {}  # cc65
